<https://w3id.org/eudpp/standard/sustainability/16> a eudpp:SustainabilityStandard ;
  dct:description "* The ETI Base Code is a code of labor practice, derived from the Conventions of the ILO. * The code is generic, meaning that it is applicable for any type of company, in any place in the world. * Companies can become members of ETI, and as such they adopt the Base Code and commit to making sure their suppliers work towards full compliance over time. * The Base Code is mostly applied for managing labour practices in international supply chains. * ETI does not certify companies or products against the code, and does not encourage others to do so. ETI does require, though, that members demonstrate that they continuously work towards compliance and that they report openly on their progress to ETI. * ETI is a stakeholders governed organization, having business, trade union and NGO's as it main stakeholders. Companies, trade unions and NGO's can all become members. ETI together with its members and other stakeholders develops implementation practices, and gives guidance on code implementation. * The code is internationally well recognized, and has served as a basis for other other initiatives and codes to build on." ;
  dct:issued "1998"^^xsd:gYear ;
  dct:relation <https://www.ethicaltrade.org/eti-base-code>, <https://www.ethicaltrade.org/sites/default/files/shared_resources/eti_human_rights_due_diligence_framework.pdf> ;
  dct:source <https://www.standardsmap.org/en/factsheet/16> ;
  dct:title "Ethical Trading Initiative  - ETI" ;
