<https://w3id.org/eudpp/standard/sustainability/202> a eudpp:SustainabilityStandard ;
  dct:description "* FTT standards and certification procedures are periodically reviewed for alignment with global best practice, exceed the requirements of South Africa's Minimum Standard for Responsible Tourism (SANS 1162:2011), and are designed to address the tourism context and development priorities characteristic of the tourism landscape in Africa.  * The FTT Standard is structured according to the following areas: A Commitment to Fair Trade Tourism implementation and communication; B Demonstrate good tourism business practice; C Maximise social and economic benefits in the local areas, and minimise negative impacts; D Maximise benefits and minimise negative impacts to cultural heritage; E Maximise benefits and minimise negative impacts through responsible environmental management practices; F Maximise benefits and minimise negative impact to biodiversity, ecosystems and landscapes. Sections G and H apply to the responsible management and impacts of volunteer tourism and community tours respectively.   * To be eligible for certification, an applicant must meet FTT's definition of a tourism product. According to this definition, a tourism product may be formal or informal and it must provide facilities, services and/or experiences directly to tourists. This typically includes accommodation, activities, attractions, F&B services, community tours, and volunteer tourism." ;
  dct:issued "2004"^^xsd:gYear ;
  dct:relation <https://www.fairtradetourism.org/join> ;
  dct:source <https://www.standardsmap.org/en/factsheet/202> ;
  dct:title "Fair Trade Tourism Product Certification Standard - FTT" ;
